derivations.claim:
No global potential. Suppose there were a $C^{2}$ function $U$ on $Q$ with $F=-
abla U$. The line integral of [L2] is linear in the field by its definition as a sum of parameter integrals, so $\int_{\gamma}F\cdot d\mathbf r=-\int_{\gamma}
abla U\cdot d\mathbf r$; the gradient theorem of [L2] applied to the $C^{1}$ function $U$ gives $\int_{\gamma}
abla U\cdot d\mathbf r=U(\gamma(2\pi))-U(\gamma(0))=U(L_{0},0,0)-U(L_{0},0,0)=0$ because $\gamma$ is closed. Hence $\int_{\gamma}F\cdot d\mathbf r=0$, contradicting the value $2\pi eta
eq0$ of step 2.3. Therefore no such global potential exists, the model is not a global potential model in the sense of clause (a) of [[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]], and no global mechanical energy $K+U$ of the framework's form is available for it.
